  "comments": "House of Windsor (1917 to date), Elizabeth II (1952-2022), Pre-decimal issues (1953-70).<br /><br />\r\nThe original weight of these coins was 2/11 troy ounce (87.3 grains). Under the Coinage Act of 1946, the composition changed from .500 silver to .750 copper/.250 nickel but the weight remained at 87.3 grains.<br /><br />\r\n<span style=\"font-style:italic;\">1955 reverse die varieties:</span><br />\r\n<span style=\"font-weight:bold;\">Large design</span> Narrower rim, thinner teeth, characteristic angular appearance;<br />\r\n<span style=\"font-weight:bold;\">Small design</span> Broader rim, thicker teeth, characteristic rounded appearance;<br />\r\n<a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/5cc5760035eef.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/5cc5760035eef.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"200\" height=\"150\" /></a><br />\r\n<span style=\"font-style:italic;\">1956 reverse die varieties:</span><br />\r\n<span style=\"font-weight:bold;\">Rev C</span> D of FID between beads, I of FID at a bead, D of DEF at a bead, right quatrefoil at a bead (smaller beads);<br />\r\n<span style=\"font-weight:bold;\">Rev D</span> D of FID to left of bead, I of FID between beads, D of DEF to left of bead, right quatrefoil to right of bead (larger beads);<br />\r\n<a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/574bf0c0bccf3.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/574bf0c0bccf3.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"196\" height=\"200\" /></a><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/574bf12575836.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/574bf12575836.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"197\" height=\"200\" /></a><br />\r\n<a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/5c838dc16072e.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/5c838dc16072e.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"200\" height=\"150\" /></a><br />\r\nImages above are reproduced with kind permission of Declan Magee and Numista member Sjoelund.<br /><br />\r\nThe 1970-dated proof strikes were issued as part of the &#039;Last Sterling&#039; mint set which comprised<br />\r\nhalf crown to halfpenny and were produced over the course of several years:<br />\r\n01/04/71-31/03/72 = 69,763<br />\r\n01/04/72-31/03/73 = 586,554<br />\r\n01/04/73-31/03/74 = 74,293<br />\r\n01/04/74-31/03/75 = 19,814",
